\section{Detailed proof of Exercise 1.31}
\setcounter{Exercise}{30}
useenv exec {
    Define
    $$
        \inf\emptyset = oo \quad"and"\quad \sup\emptyset = -oo.
    $$
    Show that every subset of $\overline\R$ has a greatest-lower-bound.
}

useenv pf {
    By definition, the empty set has the greatest lower bound. Thus it suffices
    to consider for non-empty sets.
    Fix $E$ a non-empty subset of $\overline\R$, and let $L$ be a collection of
    all lower bounds of $E$ at $\overline\R$.
    We need following facts for the set $L$.
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item \fbox{$-oo\in L$. Thus $L$ is not empty.}\break\vskip-11pt
        This is clear because $-oo$ is the smallest element of $\overline\R$,
        thus it is indeed a lower bound of $E$.

        \item \fbox{If $a\in L$, then for every $b<=a$, $b\in L$. In other words, if
        $a\in L$, $[-oo,a] \subset L$.}\break\vskip-11pt
        Recall that $a\in L$ means that $a$ is a lower bound of $E$, that is,
        $e>=a$ for every $e\in E$. If $b <= a$, then by the definition of the
        ordering,\footnote{
            I taught the term \lq\lq total ordering\rq\rq\ in the class.
            But as the lecture note does not use that term but use \lq\lq
            ordering\rq\rq\ instead, I wrote \lq\lq ordering\rq\rq\ also in
            here.
        } we have $e >= a >= b$, thus $b$ is also a lower bound of $E$. This
        gives $b\in L$.

        \item \fbox{If the least-upper-bound of $L$ exists, then $\sup L \in
        L$.}\break\vskip-11pt
        Indeed, let $\alpha$ be the least-upper-bound of $L$. If $\alpha\notin
        L$, that means that $\alpha$ is not a lower bound of $E$.
        In this case, there exists $e\in E$ such that $e < \alpha$.
        Since $\alpha$ is the least-upper-bound of $L$, there exists $\beta\in
        L$ such that $e < \beta <= \alpha$.
        However, notice that $L$ is a collection of all lower bounds of $E$,
        $\beta$ is chosen in $L$, but we obtain $e < \beta$, which is a
        contradiction.
        Therefore, $\alpha\in L$, and (iii) is now proved.

        \item \fbox{If the least-upper-bound of $L$ exists, then the
        greatest-lower-bound of $E$ exists, and $\sup L = \inf
        E$.}\break\vskip-11pt
        Indeed, by (iii), $\sup L\in L$. This implies that $\sup L$ is also a
        lower bound of $E$. Now fix any $\gamma\in\overline\R$ such that $\gamma
        > \sup L$. From (ii), if $\gamma\in L$, then we have
        $[-oo,\gamma]\subset L$. Especially, $(\sup L,\gamma] \subset L$, and
        indeed $(\sup L,\gamma]$ is not empty. This contradicts with the fact
        that $\sup L$ is the least-upper-bound of $L$.

        What we showed is that if $\gamma > \sup L$, then $\gamma\notin L$.
        This means that $\gamma$ is not a lower bound of $E$, proving that $E$
        has a greatest-lower-bound, and $\sup L = \inf E$. In fact, we actually
        proves that $L = [-oo,\sup L]$ if $L$ has the least-upper-bound.
    }

    From the fact (iv), the proof of exercise is finished if we show that $L$
    has the least-upper-bound. Because of (i), it suffices to consider following
    three cases.

    #enum (Case \Roman*.) {
        \item \fbox{$L = \{-oo\}$}\break\vskip-11pt
        There is nothing to prove in this case because indeed, $\sup L = -oo$.

        \item \fbox{$oo \in L$}\break\vskip-11pt
        From the fact (ii), in this case, we actually have $L = [-oo,oo] =
        \overline\R$. Hence, $L$ has the least-upper-bound, and $\sup L = oo$.

        \item \fbox{There exist two real numbers $p$ and $q$ such that $p < q$,
        $p\in L$, and $q\notin L$}\break\vskip-11pt
        In this case, consider $\tilde L = L \cap \R$.
        Since $p$ is a real number with an element of $E$, $p\in\tilde L$.
        In other words, $\tilde L$ is not empty.
        In addition, $q$ is an upper bound of $\tilde L$. Otherwise, there
        exists $r\in\tilde L$ such that $r > q$.
        Then by (ii), we have $q \in [-oo,r] \subset L$, which is a
        contradiction with $q\notin L$.

        What we shown is that $\tilde L$ is a non-empty subset of $\R$ which is
        bounded above. By the least-upper-bound property of $\R$, $\tilde L$ has
        the least-upper-bound and (obviously) $\sup\tilde L\in\R$.

        We finaly claim that $\sup\tilde L$ is the least-upper-bound of $L$.
        To write simply, put $\alpha = \sup\tilde L$.
        If there exists $\beta\in L$ such that $\beta > \alpha$,
        first of all, $\beta$ is a real number. Indeed, $\beta$ is larger than
        $\alpha$, so $\beta$ cannot be $-oo$. In addition, since there is a real
        number $q$ which does not contained in $L$, $\beta$ also cannot be $oo$.
        Hence, $\beta\in\tilde L$. But then we directly obtain a contradiction
        because $\alpha$ is the least-upper-bound of $\tilde L$.
        Therefore, we proved that $\beta<=\alpha$ for every $\beta\in L$;
        $\alpha$ is an upper bound of $L$.

        Now fix any $\gamma < \alpha$. If $\gamma = -oo$, we clearly have
        $\gamma < p <= \alpha$.
        As $\alpha$ is a real number, $\gamma$ cannot be $oo$, thus we can
        further assume that $\gamma$ is a real number.
        But then since $\alpha$ is the least-upper-bound of $\tilde L$, there
        exists $r\in\tilde L$ such that $\gamma < r <= \alpha$.
        By the definition of $\tilde L$, $r$ is clearly an element of $L$.
        Therefore, we proved that $\alpha$ is the least-upper-bound of $L$.
    }
    The proof is now finished. \proved
}

\section{The proof of Exercise 1.27}
\setcounter{section}{1}
\setcounter{Exercise}{26}
useenv exec {
    Let $F$ and $K$ be ordered fields which have the least-upper-bound
    property and contain $\Q$. Show that we can identify $F$ and $K$ in a
    certain sense. In other words, show that $\R$ is the unique ordered field
    which has the least-upper-bound property and contains $\Q$ in a certain
    sense.
}

Before solving this exercise, we should justify what does the \lq\lq certain
sense\rq\rq\ means mathematically.
For example, consider two sets: $\Q$, a collection of all rational numbers, and
a set $Q$ defined by
$$
    Q = \{ q\sqrt{2} : q\in\Q \}.
$$
We want to define a new addition, multiplication for $Q$ by (to
distinguish of which in $\Q$, we shall write $\tilde+$, and $\tilde\cdot$
for an addition, a multiplication on $Q$, respectively)
#def #add {\mathbin{\tilde+}}
#def #mul {\mathbin{\tilde\cdot}}
$$
    (a\sqrt{2}) #add (b\sqrt{2}) = (a + b)\sqrt{2},\quad
    (a\sqrt{2}) #mul (b\sqrt{2}) = (ab)\sqrt{2}.
$$
It may seem strange because in high school we learn that
$(a\sqrt{2})\cdot(b\sqrt{2}) = 2ab$. But this is a different mathematical setting.
The set $Q$ here is being treated with a number system which is different from
the one we are used to.

In that definiton, the set $Q$ is then just a collection of rational numbers
with special suffix $\sqrt{2}$. More mathematically, if we define a function $\Phi:\Q->Q$
by
$$
    \Phi(q) = q\sqrt{2},
$$
then by definition (and the fact that $\sqrt{2}$ is irrational), $\Phi$ is one-to-one
and onto function together with
$$
    \Phi(x + y) = \Phi(x) #add \Phi(y),\quad \Phi(xy) = \Phi(x) #mul \Phi(y).
$$

Thus, mathematically, the meaning of \lq\lq certain sense\rq\rq\ is that there exists
an one-to-one function $\Phi$ from $F$ onto $K$ which \lq\lq preserves\rq\rq\
the addition, the multiplication, and the order. That is,
$$
    \Phi(x +_F y) = \Phi(x) +_K \Phi(y),\quad
    \Phi(x \cdot_F y) = \Phi(x) \cdot_K \Phi(y),
$$
and $x <_F y$ implies $\Phi(x) <_K \Phi(y)$.
Here, the subscript under $+$, $\cdot$, and $<$ denotes the set where operations
are performed.
By the way, such $\Phi$ is called an \it{isomorphism}.

Now we are ready to prove the exercise.

useenv pf {
  By the above observation, it suffices to find such function $\Phi$.
}

